WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
A goldfish in a bowl soaks up water all day, and a cod in the sea loses it.

2
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.280
How does each one stay the same size?

3
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:15.433
Start with one rule, for a surface water can cross.

4
00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:21.093
With nothing pushing back, water moves on its own toward the side with more

5
00:00:21.093 --> 00:00:22.093
dissolved particles.

6
00:00:23.413 --> 00:00:25.153
That count is called osmolality.

7
00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:29.773
In these fish, each salt leaks toward the side where it is scarcer.

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.327
Watch the bar in the corner of every tank.

9
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:38.887
One mark shows the body's osmolality, and one shows the water outside.

10
00:00:40.387 --> 00:00:42.007
Water drifts toward the higher mark.

11
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Now give the fish a ledger: one column for what comes in, one for what goes out.

12
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.360
If its water and salts hold steady, the columns must match, for water and for each

13
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.360
salt separately.

14
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:58.243
Drop a bony fish into the sea.

15
00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:03.063
Its blood holds far fewer dissolved particles than seawater.

16
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:07.463
So water leaks out across its gills, and salt leaks in.

17
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:11.187
Water is short, so the fish drinks seawater.

18
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:15.687
Its gut absorbs the water, but the salt comes in with it.

19
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.370
Now salt is the problem.

20
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.370
Cells in the gills send sodium and chloride back out into the sea.

21
00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.190
That runs against the leak, so it costs ATP.

22
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.320
Now move a bony fish to river.

23
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.200
The bar flips. Its blood is far saltier than fresh water.

24
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.580
So water pours in across the gills, and salt leaks out.

25
00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.323
The water is easy to fix.

26
00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:49.303
The kidney makes a large volume of dilute urine, and the extra water leaves that

27
00:01:49.303 --> 00:01:50.303
way.

28
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:55.307
But that urine carries a little salt away, and salt keeps leaking out across the

29
00:01:55.307 --> 00:01:58.667
gills. What must the gills now do with salt?

30
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:11.273
Take it in. Gill cells pull sodium and chloride from the river water, against the

31
00:02:11.273 --> 00:02:14.213
leak. Again, that costs ATP.

32
00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:21.763
One more tank. A shark lives in the same sea as the cod, and its blood holds less

33
00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:22.883
salt than seawater.

34
00:02:24.223 --> 00:02:25.423
Yet it does not lose water.

35
00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:27.763
Why not?

36
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:33.797
Its blood also carries a large load of urea, which the shark makes itself and holds

37
00:02:33.797 --> 00:02:37.617
on to. A little TMAO rides along.

38
00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:40.737
Both count as dissolved particles.

39
00:02:42.137 --> 00:02:44.437
With them, the blood sits just above seawater.

40
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:49.377
So the water leak runs the other way, as a slow trickle inward.

41
00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.277
The shark need not drink seawater.

42
00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.970
Three tanks, one budget.

43
00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.550
The gap between body and water sets which way the leaks run.

44
00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.930
Drinking, urine and paid salt transport close each gap.
